<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samudragupta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 335–375 CE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samudragupta was an emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> who reigned c. 335–375 CE. He transformed the kingdom he inherited from his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-i">Chandragupta I</a> into a pan-Indian empire through campaigns recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allahabad-pillar-inscription">Allahabad Pillar Inscription</a>. The Gupta Empire profile notes his distinction between northern kingdoms that were conquered and absorbed and southern rulers who were defeated and then reinstated as tributaries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samudragupta.jpg" alt="Samudragupta" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SamudraguptaCoin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
